{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.6", "U10.7", "U11.6", "U11.7"],
      "nets": ["CAN_P", "CAN_N"],
      "obs": "The two isolated CAN transceivers, U11 (driven by CAN0 controller U8) and U10 (driven by CAN1 controller U9), both have their CANH/CANL outputs connected to the same global nets labelled \"CAN_P\" and \"CAN_N\". The two buses are therefore shorted together on a single pair of nets.",
      "exp": "The specification requires two separate isolated buses: \"two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9)\" and TVS arrays \"on CAN0_P/N, CAN1_P/N\". U11 should drive CAN0_P/CAN0_N and U10 should drive CAN1_P/CAN1_N, kept apart.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "CAN BUS sheet: U11 pin7 (CANH)/pin6 (CANL) -> global labels \"CAN_P\"/\"CAN_N\"; U10 pin7/pin6 -> the same global labels \"CAN_P\"/\"CAN_N\""},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9)"}
      ],
      "impact": "CAN0 and CAN1 are merged into one physical bus; the two isolated domains are no longer isolated and will fight each other, corrupting motor-communication traffic.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["J2", "J13"],
      "pins": ["J2.2", "J2.3", "J2.5", "J2.8", "J13.2", "J13.3", "J13.5", "J13.8"],
      "nets": ["CAN0_P", "CAN0_N", "CAN1_P", "CAN1_N"],
      "obs": "The M12 connectors J2 (\"CAN Interface A\") and J13 (\"CAN Interface B\") carry the indexed nets CAN0_P/CAN0_N and CAN1_P/CAN1_N, but no transceiver drives those names (the transceivers are on the un-indexed CAN_P/CAN_N nets). The connector CAN pairs are therefore floating.",
      "exp": "Per the spec the transceiver outputs must reach the M12 connectors under matching net names (CAN0_P/CAN1_P etc.), as in the reference design.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Connector sheet: J2 pin5=\"CAN0_P\", pin8=\"CAN0_N\", pin2=\"CAN1_P\", pin3=\"CAN1_N\"; J13 pin5=\"CAN0_P\", pin8=\"CAN0_N\", pin2=\"CAN1_P\", pin3=\"CAN1_N\" (no transceiver label matches these)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "broken out on M12 circular connectors J2 (\"CAN Interface A\") and J13 (\"CAN Interface B\")"}
      ],
      "impact": "Neither CAN bus reaches the Odrive motor connectors; motor control communication is lost.",
      "confidence": 0.65
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The CAN physical-layer global labels are inconsistent with the reference design: both transceivers U10 and U11 are tied to the same un-indexed nets CAN_P/CAN_N (merging the two isolated buses), while the M12 connectors J2/J13 use the indexed nets CAN0_P/CAN1_P/CAN0_N/CAN1_N that nothing drives. This bridges CAN0 and CAN1 together and leaves the motor connectors unconnected, breaking the board's primary motor-communication function."
}

